        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>Syslog</strong> is a standardized mechanism used by network devices (firewalls, routers, switches) and servers to send event and log messages to a centralized collector or logging server.</p>
            
            <h3>Message Components</h3>
            <p>A standard syslog message contains several parts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Timestamp:</strong> When the event occurred.</li>
                <li><strong>Hostname / Source:</strong> Which device sent it.</li>
                <li><strong>Facility:</strong> The subsystem that generated the log (e.g., kernel, mail, auth).</li>
                <li><strong>Severity:</strong> How critical the event is.</li>
                <li><strong>Message:</strong> The actual log content.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Severity Levels</h3>
            <p>Syslog uses a numerical severity scale where a <strong>lower numerical value indicates a more severe event.</strong></p>
            <ol start="0">
                <li><strong>Emergency (0):</strong> System is unusable.</li>
                <li><strong>Alert (1):</strong> Action must be taken immediately.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical (2):</strong> Critical conditions.</li>
                <li><strong>Error (3):</strong> Error conditions.</li>
                <li><strong>Warning (4):</strong> Warning conditions.</li>
                <li><strong>Notice (5):</strong> Normal but significant condition.</li>
                <li><strong>Informational (6):</strong> Informational messages.</li>
                <li><strong>Debug (7):</strong> Debug-level messages.</li>
            </ol>
            
            <h3>Transports and Security</h3>
            <p>Traditional syslog commonly relies on <strong>UDP port 514</strong>. Because UDP is connectionless, logs can be lost in transit. Some implementations support TCP 514 for reliability. Secure syslog over TLS commonly uses <strong>TCP 6514</strong>, providing encryption to protect sensitive log data from interception.</p>
            <p><em>Note: Log collection is not full monitoring. Logs complement active <a href="snmp-explained.html">SNMP</a> metrics and alerts.</em></p>
        
